\subsection{Lists from Trees}

\begin{note}
Structural recursion on a tree is the same idea as on a linked list,
except that there are two recursive calls instead of one.
For a tree \lstinline{t} that may be \lstinline{None}, the pattern is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the base case is \lstinline{t is None}, and
\item the recursive case combines the result of recursing on \lstinline{t.left},
    the current value \lstinline{t.val},
    and the result of recursing on \lstinline{t.right}.
\end{enumerate}
The \emph{order} of those three pieces determines the traversal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{inorder}: left, value, right---produces sorted order.
\item \textbf{preorder}: value, left, right.
\item \textbf{postorder}: left, right, value.
\end{enumerate}
\end{note}

\begin{note}
To \emph{insert} into a tree, we descend using the BST invariant
and rebuild the tree on the way back up.
This is why \lstinline{insert} must return the new subtree root:
when the descent reaches \lstinline{None} we return a newly-created node,
and each ancestor reassigns its child pointer to the returned value.
Forgetting the reassignment \lstinline{t.left = insert(t.left, val)}
silently throws away every node except the root.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
Once we can produce the sorted list via an inorder traversal,
the list-like interface is trivial:
\lstinline{__getitem__(i)} is just \lstinline{inorder(self.root)[i]}.
Negative indexing and out-of-range \lstinline{IndexError} come for free from list semantics.
\end{note}
